// THE DEFECT THIS MODULE CLOSES IS A PLACEMENT DEFECT, NOT AN ADJUDICATION ONE.
//
// gunbc.live_deploy.repository_convergence is the git-native transition -- import objects, compare
// and swap the base ref, reset the worktree, read back. It is correct and it is unreachable, because
// nothing in this repository can say WHERE IT RUNS. The fleet-converge job executes
// `gunbc run ... --function live_deploy_apply_srv1_wet` as the UNPRIVILEGED CI-RUNNER account, and
// /opt/gunbc/gunbc is owned by the SERVICE USER. A converge invoked from that process is one
// principal mutating another principal's repository, which is the arrangement that already failed
// once by measurement: the first flip deploy (run 29822498755, 2026-07-21) died on bare-rsync
// EACCES and chgrp EPERM into that exact tree, and the repair was to stop having the runner write it.
//
// THE PLACEMENT IS DERIVED FROM THE GRANT ROSTER, NOT CHOSEN FOR TASTE. gunbc.fleet_posix_accounts
// grants the deploy principal exactly six NOPASSWD commands -- apt-get, install, visudo, systemctl,
// tailscale, rm. `git` is not among them and neither is any `sudo -u` form, so THERE IS NO PRIMITIVE
// IN THE ADMITTED SET THAT RUNS AN ARBITRARY COMMAND AS ANOTHER USER. What is granted is `install`
// and `systemctl`, and a systemd oneshot unit carrying `User=` is what those two compose into. That
// is not an analogy: gunbc-tree-sync.service is the same construction, installed for the same reason
// (emit_deploy_principal_note: "the owner writes its own tree"), and it is the existing proof that
// the route works and that the service user can READ the runner's checkout.
//
// SO THE CHOICE WAS MADE BY THE ROSTER. Any other placement -- a helper binary, a sudo widening, an
// ssh hop back into the host -- requires granting the deploy principal something it does not have,
// and the grant roster is a fleet authority rather than this lane's to widen.

// WHY THE CORPUS COMES FROM THE RUNNER CHECKOUT AND NOT FROM THE TARGET.
//
// The deployed tree is 637 commits behind the admitted revision, which is the condition being
// repaired, so its checked-out `.dag` corpus PREDATES gunbc.live_deploy.repository_convergence
// entirely. A unit that ran the target's own corpus would be asking a tree from 2026-08-19 to
// perform an operation first authored on 2026-08-27. The corpus must therefore come from the
// workflow revision, and the workflow revision is materialized on the host already: the runner's
// checkout, whose absolute path the deploy writes into the tree-sync environment file as
// GUNBC_TREE_SRC.
//
// THE BINARY IS THE ADMITTED ONE FOR A DIFFERENT REASON AND IT MUST NOT BE CONFLATED. srv1 carries
// no Cargo toolchain, so nothing can be built there; the binary the unit executes is the one THIS
// deploy just installed at spec.service.serve_binary from the runner's build of the same revision.
// Binary and corpus are two facts that must both come from the workflow revision, and they arrive by
// two different routes -- one installed, one read in place.

// WHY THE OBJECTS ARE FETCHED FROM THE RUNNER CHECKOUT RATHER THAN FROM `origin`.
//
// gunbc.deployed_tree_remote records that srv1's configured remote exists solely so
// `git ls-remote origin` can answer, that the tree PERFORMS NO FETCH, and that `.git/config` is
// excluded from the sync because a blanket copy was once found carrying a live Actions
// extraheader token. So the target holds a public HTTPS URL and no credential, and fetching a bare
// commit id over that transport depends on the server's upload-pack configuration rather than on
// anything this repository controls.
//
// The runner checkout is a LOCAL repository already holding exactly the admitted revision. Fetching
// from it needs no network, no credential, and no server-side option, and -- the load-bearing part --
// it cannot resolve the candidate against a DIFFERENT population than the one the deploy admitted,
// because it is that population. A fetch from `origin` would reintroduce a second source of truth
// for the admitted subject at the last step of the transaction.
//
// THIS DOES NOT CONTRADICT THAT MODULE'S NO-FETCH RULING, and the distinction is exact: its concern
// is a fetch REFSPEC written into config, which would make a bare `git fetch origin` clobber
// refs/remotes/origin/*. The convergence fetches an explicit commit id from an explicit path and
// writes FETCH_HEAD only; the base ref moves under its own compare-and-swap.

// THE OPERANDS ARE DERIVED, AND THE TWO THAT ARE NOT ARE THE TWO THAT CANNOT BE.
//
// repository_converge_wet takes four strings and a caller may hand it any four. The exposure runs
// through repo and base_ref, where a wrong value would converge some other repository or advance
// some other ref while reporting success about the one asked for.
//
// So this module does not take operands. It DERIVES repo and base_ref from the deployment spec,
// which is the authority for where this instance's tree lives. Two values remain arguments because
// no authoring-time fact determines them: the CANDIDATE, which is the admitted subject of this
// particular deployment, and the OBJECT SOURCE, which is the path the runner happened to check out
// on this particular run. Both are rendered into the unit by convergence_unit_doc from the spec and
// the deploy's own environment file, so the production path never hand-spells either.
type ConvergencePlacementOperands {
  repo: String
  remote: String
  base_ref: String
  candidate: String
}

// THE PRIMARY IS NO LONGER THIS MODULE'S TO DERIVE. This record carried a primary_path alongside
// repo, read from the same spec field, because repository_converge_wet took the primary worktree
// path as a caller string and a wrong one exempts the wrong worktree. #9555 moved that derivation
// into the callee, which now reads the primary from git's own roster -- the stronger construction,
// and the one this block already named as belonging to the module that owns the adjudication
// (extdeps.git.worktree_enumeration declares the primary/linked split for it). The field went with
// the argument rather than outliving it: kept, it would have been derived here, witnessed here, and
// read by nobody.
data convergence_base_ref: String = "refs/remotes/origin/main"

type ConvergenceOperandAdmission
  = ConvergenceOperandsAdmitted { operands: ConvergencePlacementOperands }
  | ConvergenceOperandsRefused { reason: String }

// A COMMIT ID IS DECODED BY GIT'S OWN AUTHORITY, NEVER SHAPE-CHECKED HERE.
//
// Every downstream consumer takes the candidate as a string, so an empty or ABBREVIATED value would
// reach `git fetch` and `update-ref` as something git may well resolve -- and an abbreviation
// resolves against whatever is unambiguous in the TARGET's object store today, which is a different
// population from the one that admitted the revision. The admitted subject is a full object id or it
// is not the admitted subject.
//
// THE CHECK IS NOT WRITTEN HERE BECAUSE IT ALREADY EXISTS. extdeps.git.object_store
// git_decode_object_id_text is git's cited decoder and it distinguishes exactly the three refusals
// this operand needs -- wrong length, non-hexadecimal, and non-canonical uppercase -- returning a
// GitObjectId rather than a string that passed a predicate. A local `is_lowercase_hex` beside it
// would have been a second authority for one concept (DESIGN section 3), and the weaker one: it
// would have answered Bool where this answers WHICH WAY the value is not an object id.
fn candidate_refusal_text(candidate: String, cause: GitObjectIdTextRefusal) -> String {
  match cause {
    GitObjectIdTextWrongLength { format: _, observed: n } =>
      join([
        "the candidate revision `", candidate, "` is ", to_string(value: n),
        " characters; a full object id is required, because an abbreviation resolves against the",
        " TARGET's object store rather than against the population that admitted it",
      ], "")
    GitObjectIdTextNonHexadecimal =>
      join(["the candidate revision `", candidate, "` is not hexadecimal"], "")
    GitObjectIdTextNonCanonicalUppercase =>
      join(["the candidate revision `", candidate, "` carries uppercase hex; git's canonical",
            " spelling is lowercase, and admitting both would let one commit have two names"], "")
  }
}

fn convergence_operands_for(
  spec: DeploymentSpec,
  candidate: String,
  object_source: String,
) -> ConvergenceOperandAdmission {
  let repo = spec.service.repo_root as String
  match git_decode_object_id_text(format: GitObjectFormatSha1, text: candidate) {
    GitObjectIdTextRefused { cause: cause } =>
      ConvergenceOperandsRefused {
        reason: candidate_refusal_text(candidate: candidate, cause: cause),
      }
    GitObjectIdTextDecoded { oid: oid } =>
      if trim(s: object_source) == "" {
        ConvergenceOperandsRefused {
          reason: "the object source is empty -- GUNBC_TREE_SRC did not reach the unit, so the admitted revision's objects have no route into the target",
        }
      } else if !starts_with(s: object_source, prefix: "/") {
        ConvergenceOperandsRefused {
          reason: join([
            "the object source `", object_source, "` is not absolute; the unit runs with a working",
            " directory of its own, so a relative object source names a path nobody chose",
          ], ""),
        }
      } else {
        ConvergenceOperandsAdmitted { operands: ConvergencePlacementOperands {
          repo: repo,
          remote: object_source,
          base_ref: convergence_base_ref,
          candidate: git_object_id_wire_hex(oid: oid) as String,
        } }
      }
  }
}

// THE PRINCIPAL IS OBSERVED, NEVER ASSUMED, AND THE UNOBSERVABLE CASE IS NOT THE ADMITTING ONE.
//
// This is the wall the brief asks for: a placement that cannot establish it is running as the right
// principal must refuse. The three states are genuinely three -- observed-and-correct,
// observed-and-wrong, and could-not-observe -- and collapsing the third into either of the others is
// the empty-observation narrow. Wrong tells an operator the unit is misconfigured; unobservable
// tells them `whoami` did not answer, which is a different repair on a different machine state.
//
// IT FIRES ON THE PATH THAT MATTERS. If someone invokes this entry from the fleet-converge job --
// the exact mistake this module exists to prevent, and the one an author reaching for the shortest
// route would make -- the observed principal is the CI runner, not the service user, and the
// transition refuses before a single git command runs.
type ConvergencePrincipalAdmission
  = ConvergencePrincipalAdmitted { name: String }
  | ConvergencePrincipalMismatch { expected: String, observed: String }
  | ConvergencePrincipalUnobservable { reason: String }

fn convergence_principal_admission(
  expected: String,
  result: EffectivePosixPrincipalObservationResult,
) -> ConvergencePrincipalAdmission {
  match result {
    EffectivePosixPrincipalObservationRefused { read: _, reason: why } =>
      ConvergencePrincipalUnobservable { reason: why }
    EffectivePosixPrincipalObserved { observation: obs } => {
      let observed = effective_posix_principal_name(observation: obs) as String
      if observed == expected {
        ConvergencePrincipalAdmitted { name: observed }
      } else {
        ConvergencePrincipalMismatch { expected: expected, observed: observed }
      }
    }
  }
}

// WHAT THE OUTER TRANSACTION RECEIVES, AND WHY IT IS NOT AN EXIT CODE.
//
// The convergence runs in a DIFFERENT PROCESS from the deploy that ordered it -- that is the whole
// content of this module -- and `systemctl start` reports whether the unit's control process
// terminated zero. The deploy therefore learns "the unit failed" and nothing about WHICH of the
// eleven states the adjudication distinguishes it reached, which would hand a CAS race, an
// unreadable worktree roster and a wrong principal the same response. So the unit writes a typed
// receipt and the deploy decodes it.
//
// THERE IS NO exit_code FIELD, AND ITS ABSENCE IS DERIVED RATHER THAN ECONOMISED. convergence_exit
// maps every non-converged outcome to ExitFailure { code: 1 }, so the code is a function of the
// converged/not distinction the verdict already carries -- storing it would be a second
// representation of one fact (DESIGN section 2), and a reader who saw both could not be told which
// one to believe if they ever disagreed.
type ConvergencePlacementReceipt
  = PlacementWrongPrincipal { expected: String, observed: String }
  | PlacementPrincipalUnobservable { reason: String }
  | PlacementOperandsRefused { reason: String }
  | PlacementConverged { candidate: String, repo: String }
  | PlacementConvergenceRefused { candidate: String, repo: String, detail: String }

// THE ONE PREDICATE AN ACTUATOR MAY ASK. Every non-converged arm is false, including the two that
// never reached git at all: a placement that refused is not a repository that converged, and a
// caller that treated "we never ran" as "nothing to do" would proceed against the stale tree this
// whole lane exists to replace.
fn placement_converged(receipt: ConvergencePlacementReceipt) -> Bool {
  match receipt {
    PlacementConverged { candidate: _, repo: _ } => true
    PlacementWrongPrincipal { expected: _, observed: _ } => false
    PlacementPrincipalUnobservable { reason: _ } => false
    PlacementOperandsRefused { reason: _ } => false
    PlacementConvergenceRefused { candidate: _, repo: _, detail: _ } => false
  }
}

fn placement_receipt_verdict(receipt: ConvergencePlacementReceipt) -> String {
  match receipt {
    PlacementConverged { candidate: _, repo: _ } => "converged"
    PlacementWrongPrincipal { expected: _, observed: _ } => "wrong-principal"
    PlacementPrincipalUnobservable { reason: _ } => "principal-unobservable"
    PlacementOperandsRefused { reason: _ } => "operands-refused"
    PlacementConvergenceRefused { candidate: _, repo: _, detail: _ } => "convergence-refused"
  }
}

fn placement_receipt_line(receipt: ConvergencePlacementReceipt) -> String {
  match receipt {
    PlacementConverged { candidate: c, repo: r } =>
      join(["repository-convergence CONVERGED repo=", r, " candidate=", c,
            " -- the service principal performed the transition and the readback agreed"], "")
    PlacementWrongPrincipal { expected: e, observed: o } =>
      join(["repository-convergence REFUSED wrong-principal expected=", e, " observed=", o,
            " -- the convergence was invoked by an account that does not own the deployed",
            " repository; it must run through the service-user unit, not in the deploy process"], "")
    PlacementPrincipalUnobservable { reason: why } =>
      join(["repository-convergence REFUSED principal-unobservable -- ", why,
            " (an unobserved principal is not an admitted one)"], "")
    PlacementOperandsRefused { reason: why } =>
      join(["repository-convergence REFUSED operands -- ", why], "")
    PlacementConvergenceRefused { candidate: c, repo: r, detail: d } =>
      join(["repository-convergence REFUSED repo=", r, " candidate=", c, " -- ", d], "")
  }
}

// THE RECEIPT IS JSON BECAUSE JSON IS ALREADY MODELLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. extdeps.languages.json
// carries serialize_json and parse_json_document against one cited grammar (RFC 8259), so writing
// the receipt and reading it back are the same rows read forwards and backwards rather than an
// emitter and a parser that can drift (DESIGN section 4). A bespoke line format would have been a
// second grammar with one author and no authority.
data placement_receipt_schema: String = "gunbc.repository-convergence-placement.v1"

fn placement_receipt_json(receipt: ConvergencePlacementReceipt) -> JsonValue {
  match receipt {
    PlacementConverged { candidate: c, repo: r } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_schema)),
        json_kv(key: "verdict", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_verdict(receipt: receipt))),
        json_kv(key: "candidate", value: json_string(s: c)),
        json_kv(key: "repo", value: json_string(s: r)),
      ])
    PlacementConvergenceRefused { candidate: c, repo: r, detail: d } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_schema)),
        json_kv(key: "verdict", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_verdict(receipt: receipt))),
        json_kv(key: "candidate", value: json_string(s: c)),
        json_kv(key: "repo", value: json_string(s: r)),
        json_kv(key: "detail", value: json_string(s: d)),
      ])
    PlacementWrongPrincipal { expected: e, observed: o } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_schema)),
        json_kv(key: "verdict", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_verdict(receipt: receipt))),
        json_kv(key: "expected", value: json_string(s: e)),
        json_kv(key: "observed", value: json_string(s: o)),
      ])
    PlacementPrincipalUnobservable { reason: why } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_schema)),
        json_kv(key: "verdict", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_verdict(receipt: receipt))),
        json_kv(key: "reason", value: json_string(s: why)),
      ])
    PlacementOperandsRefused { reason: why } =>
      json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_schema)),
        json_kv(key: "verdict", value: json_string(s: placement_receipt_verdict(receipt: receipt))),
        json_kv(key: "reason", value: json_string(s: why)),
      ])
  }
}

fn placement_receipt_text(receipt: ConvergencePlacementReceipt) -> String {
  concat(serialize_json(v: placement_receipt_json(receipt: receipt)), "\n")
}

// A FIELD READ IS A THREE-STATE FACT AND DUPLICATION IS ONE OF THE THREE. RFC 8259 permits an object
// to name a member twice and does not say which wins, which extdeps.languages.json.parse reports as
// its own outcome rather than resolving -- specifically so a domain decoder that knows the field is
// an IDENTITY can refuse. `verdict` is exactly such a field: a document carrying two verdicts must
// not read as whichever one was written second.
type ReceiptFieldRead
  = ReceiptFieldPresent { value: String }
  | ReceiptFieldUnreadable { key: String, cause: String }

fn receipt_field_read(doc: JsonValue, key: String) -> ReceiptFieldRead {
  match json_object_unique_member(v: doc, key: key) {
    JsonMemberFound { value: found } =>
      ReceiptFieldPresent { value: json_string_value_or_empty(v: found) }
    JsonMemberAbsent =>
      ReceiptFieldUnreadable { key: key, cause: "absent" }
    JsonMemberDuplicated { count: n } =>
      ReceiptFieldUnreadable {
        key: key,
        cause: join(["named ", to_string(value: n), " times; RFC 8259 does not say which wins, so an",
                     " identity field carrying more than one value is refused, never resolved"], ""),
      }
    JsonMemberNotAnObject =>
      ReceiptFieldUnreadable { key: key, cause: "the receipt document is not a JSON object" }
  }
}

fn receipt_field_or_empty(doc: JsonValue, key: String) -> String {
  match receipt_field_read(doc: doc, key: key) {
    ReceiptFieldPresent { value: v } => v
    ReceiptFieldUnreadable { key: _, cause: _ } => ""
  }
}

type ConvergencePlacementReceiptDecode
  = PlacementReceiptDecoded { receipt: ConvergencePlacementReceipt }
  | PlacementReceiptUnreadable { cause: String }

// THE DECODE REFUSES ON A VERDICT IT DOES NOT RECOGNISE RATHER THAN DEFAULTING. An unknown verdict
// means the unit that wrote the file and the deploy that read it are different revisions of this
// module, and the honest answer to that is a located refusal -- the alternative, folding it into the
// nearest failure arm, would report a specific cause the document never stated.
fn placement_receipt_of_document(doc: JsonValue) -> ConvergencePlacementReceiptDecode {
  match receipt_field_read(doc: doc, key: "schema") {
    ReceiptFieldUnreadable { key: k, cause: why } =>
      PlacementReceiptUnreadable { cause: join(["receipt field `", k, "` ", why], "") }
    ReceiptFieldPresent { value: schema } =>
      if schema != placement_receipt_schema {
        PlacementReceiptUnreadable {
          cause: join(["receipt schema `", schema, "` is not `", placement_receipt_schema,
                       "`; the writer and the reader are different revisions of this module"], ""),
        }
      } else {
        match receipt_field_read(doc: doc, key: "verdict") {
          ReceiptFieldUnreadable { key: k, cause: why } =>
            PlacementReceiptUnreadable { cause: join(["receipt field `", k, "` ", why], "") }
          ReceiptFieldPresent { value: verdict } =>
            if verdict == "converged" {
              PlacementReceiptDecoded { receipt: PlacementConverged {
                candidate: receipt_field_or_empty(doc: doc, key: "candidate"),
                repo: receipt_field_or_empty(doc: doc, key: "repo"),
              } }
            } else if verdict == "convergence-refused" {
              PlacementReceiptDecoded { receipt: PlacementConvergenceRefused {
                candidate: receipt_field_or_empty(doc: doc, key: "candidate"),
                repo: receipt_field_or_empty(doc: doc, key: "repo"),
                detail: receipt_field_or_empty(doc: doc, key: "detail"),
              } }
            } else if verdict == "wrong-principal" {
              PlacementReceiptDecoded { receipt: PlacementWrongPrincipal {
                expected: receipt_field_or_empty(doc: doc, key: "expected"),
                observed: receipt_field_or_empty(doc: doc, key: "observed"),
              } }
            } else if verdict == "principal-unobservable" {
              PlacementReceiptDecoded { receipt: PlacementPrincipalUnobservable {
                reason: receipt_field_or_empty(doc: doc, key: "reason"),
              } }
            } else if verdict == "operands-refused" {
              PlacementReceiptDecoded { receipt: PlacementOperandsRefused {
                reason: receipt_field_or_empty(doc: doc, key: "reason"),
              } }
            } else {
              PlacementReceiptUnreadable {
                cause: join(["receipt verdict `", verdict, "` is not one this revision writes"], ""),
              }
            }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn decode_placement_receipt(text: String) -> ConvergencePlacementReceiptDecode {
  match parse_json_document(s: text) {
    JsonDocumentUnreadable { gap: gap } =>
      PlacementReceiptUnreadable {
        cause: join(["the receipt is ", json_document_gap_text(gap: gap)], ""),
      }
    JsonDocumentParsed { value: doc } => placement_receipt_of_document(doc: doc)
  }
}

// AN ABSENT RECEIPT IS NOT A SILENT PASS, AND IT IS THE STATE MOST LIKELY TO OCCUR. If the unit
// never started, or died before its write, the deploy finds no file -- and a reader that treated a
// missing receipt as "nothing to report" would let the transaction continue over a repository
// nobody converged. So the read failure is its own refusal, and it is not the same refusal as an
// unreadable one: nothing wrote versus something wrote badly have different repairs.
fn placement_receipt_read_refusal(path: String, error: String) -> String {
  join(["no convergence receipt at ", path,
        " -- the unit did not reach its write, so the transition is UNPERFORMED rather than",
        " unreported (", error, ")"], "")
}

// WHERE THE RECEIPT LIVES, AND WHY NOT INSIDE THE REPOSITORY BEING CONVERGED. The obvious home is
// beside the tree, and it is disqualified by the operation itself: `reset --hard` plus the
// adjudication's untracked-residue check mean a file written into that repository is either
// destroyed by the transition or reported by it as residue that refuses the transition. The
// instance receipts directory is already a deploy-owned member installed with the service user as
// owner (the belt publishes its tick receipts there), so it is writable by exactly the principal
// this unit runs as, and it survives the reset because it is outside the worktree.
fn convergence_receipt_path(instance_root: String) -> String {
  join([instance_receipts_dir(instance_root: instance_root), "/repository-convergence.json"], "")
}

// THIS MODULE'S OWN FILE PATH, CARRIED AS DATA BECAUSE THE UNIT MUST NAME IT AS A `--entry`.
//
// A source path is a POSITIONAL fact, which DESIGN section 3 admits only where no symbol names the
// thing -- and no symbol does: `--entry` takes a file, not a module. RESIDUE, named rather than
// hidden: renaming or moving this file breaks the emitted unit and nothing in the corpus refuses,
// because the fact that would catch it (module name to source path) is not modelled. That is one
// row of the same class the declaration index closed for citations, and it dissolves when an entry
// can be named by its MODULE rather than by its file.
data convergence_placement_entry_path: String = "dag/gunbc/live_deploy/repository_convergence_placement.dag"

data convergence_placement_entry_function: String = "repository_converge_srv1_owner_wet"

// THE SOURCE-ROOT ROSTER IS THE SAME PAIR EVERY OTHER INVOCATION USES -- `dag` and `src/v2` -- and it
// is spelled here against the runner checkout rather than against the target, which is the whole
// point of the placement. Every path in the command is absolute after systemd expands
// GUNBC_TREE_SRC, so the unit needs no WorkingDirectory: it inherits `/`, and nothing it runs
// resolves a relative path.
fn convergence_exec_start(
  spec: DeploymentSpec,
  candidate: String,
  receipt_path: String,
) -> String {
  let src = "$\{GUNBC_TREE_SRC\}"
  join([
    spec.service.serve_binary as String, " run",
    " --source-root ", src, "/dag",
    " --source-root ", src, "/src/v2",
    " --entry ", src, "/", convergence_placement_entry_path,
    " --function ", convergence_placement_entry_function,
    " --arg candidate=", candidate,
    " --arg object_source=", src,
    " --arg receipt=", receipt_path,
  ], "")
}

// THE UNIT IS THE PLACEMENT. `User=` is the entire mechanism -- it is what makes the repository's
// OWNER perform the transition -- and `EnvironmentFile=` is what carries the runner checkout path
// into a process the runner did not fork.
//
// THE ENVIRONMENT FILE IS TREE-SYNC'S, DELIBERATELY AND WITH A NAMED CONSEQUENCE. GUNBC_TREE_SRC is
// one fact -- where the deploy's checkout is -- and it already has one home, written by the deploy
// at spec.names.tree_sync_env_path. Writing a second file would be a second representation of it
// (DESIGN section 3). RESIDUE: that file is named for the sync this convergence replaces, so when
// the binding change deletes the rsync legs the NAME becomes a nickname for a fact whose only
// remaining consumer is this unit. Renaming it belongs to the change that deletes those legs,
// because that change owns both ends; doing it here would rename a live production file out from
// under a unit still using it.
// THE ONESHOT'S WORKING DIRECTORY IS THE RUNNER CHECKOUT, THE SAME PATH THE ENV FILE CARRIES AS
// GUNBC_TREE_SRC. The admitted binary discovers its workspace root by walking cwd's ancestors for
// .git and then requires every --source-root to sit under that root, so a unit whose cwd is `/`
// refuses (`workspace_root: / is not inside a git checkout`) and one whose cwd is the deployed
// repository refuses too (`path .../dag is not under process workspace root /opt/gunbc/gunbc`) --
// both measured on srv1 by the first deploys through `gunbc converge --mode deploy`. systemd does
// not expand environment variables in WorkingDirectory=, so the path is a parameter here and the
// writer expands it from the deploying shell's PWD, exactly as the env file's value is written.
fn convergence_unit_doc(
  spec: DeploymentSpec,
  candidate: String,
  receipt_path: String,
  working_directory: String,
) -> String {
  let user = spec.service.service_user
  serialize_systemd_unit_file(file: ServiceUnitFile {
    unit: [Description {
      text: "gunbc deployed-repository git-native convergence (service-user principal, admitted binary, runner-checkout corpus)" as NonEmptyStr,
    }],
    service: [
      ServiceType { mode: "oneshot" as NonEmptyStr },
      ServiceUser { name: user },
      ServiceGroup { name: user },
      WorkingDirectory { path: working_directory as NonEmptyStr },
      EnvironmentFile { path: spec.names.tree_sync_env_path },
      ExecStart {
        command: convergence_exec_start(
          spec: spec,
          candidate: candidate,
          receipt_path: receipt_path,
        ) as NonEmptyStr,
      },
    ],
    install: NotInstallable,
  })
}

// THE ENTRY POINT THE UNIT INVOKES, AND THE ORDER OF ITS TWO WALLS IS LOAD-BEARING.
//
// THE PRINCIPAL IS CHECKED FIRST, ahead of operand derivation, for the reason
// live_deploy_wet_with_access checks its admission ahead of the candidate observation: a process
// running as the wrong account is refusable without reference to what it was asked to do, and
// deriving operands first would make the log read as though the operands were the problem.
//
// EVERY ARM WRITES A RECEIPT, INCLUDING THE ONES THAT NEVER TOUCH GIT. A refusal that left no file
// is indistinguishable from a unit that never started, and those have different repairs -- one is a
// misconfigured placement, the other a systemd failure. The deploy must be able to tell them apart,
// so the refusals are REPORTED rather than merely exited.
func placement_finish(receipt_path: String, receipt: ConvergencePlacementReceipt) -> ProcessExit {
  let written = Filesystem.Write(
    path: receipt_path,
    content: placement_receipt_text(receipt: receipt),
  )
  if !written.success {
    exit_failure(reason: join([
      "could not write the convergence receipt to ", receipt_path, " (", written.error,
      ") -- the transition's outcome cannot reach the deploy that ordered it, so it is reported as",
      " a failure whatever the transition itself did: ", placement_receipt_line(receipt: receipt),
    ], ""))
  } else if placement_converged(receipt: receipt) {
    ExitSuccess
  } else {
    exit_failure(reason: placement_receipt_line(receipt: receipt))
  }
}

// THE CONVERGENCE'S OWN OUTCOME IS CARRIED VERBATIM, NOT RE-DECODED, AND THAT IS A DECLARED LIMIT.
//
// repository_converge_wet returns ProcessExit; its eleven-arm RepositoryConvergenceOutcome is
// consumed inside it by convergence_exit and does not cross the entry point. So this receipt is
// TOTAL about the PLACEMENT -- which principal ran, against which operands, and whether the
// transition converged -- and about the transition's cause it carries the adjudication's own
// located prose as an opaque payload. It does not branch on that prose, which would be parsing
// another module's diagnostics.
//
// RUNG: mitigatable for the transition cause, mechanically preventable for the placement.
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER for the cause: an outcome-returning sibling of repository_converge_wet, at
// which point this receipt carries the coproduct instead of the exit. That sibling belongs to the
// module that owns the adjudication, not here.
fn placement_receipt_of_convergence(
  operands: ConvergencePlacementOperands,
  exit: ProcessExit,
) -> ConvergencePlacementReceipt {
  match exit {
    ExitSuccess =>
      PlacementConverged { candidate: operands.candidate, repo: operands.repo }
    ExitFailure { code: _, reason: why } =>
      PlacementConvergenceRefused {
        candidate: operands.candidate,
        repo: operands.repo,
        detail: why,
      }
  }
}

func placement_converge_with_operands(
  receipt_path: String,
  operands: ConvergencePlacementOperands,
) -> ProcessExit {
  placement_finish(
    receipt_path: receipt_path,
    receipt: placement_receipt_of_convergence(
      operands: operands,
      exit: repository_converge_wet(
        repo: operands.repo,
        remote: operands.remote,
        base_ref: operands.base_ref,
        candidate: operands.candidate,
      ),
    ),
  )
}

func placement_converge_admitted(
  spec: DeploymentSpec,
  candidate: String,
  object_source: String,
  receipt_path: String,
) -> ProcessExit {
  match convergence_operands_for(
    spec: spec,
    candidate: candidate,
    object_source: object_source,
  ) {
    ConvergenceOperandsRefused { reason: why } =>
      placement_finish(
        receipt_path: receipt_path,
        receipt: PlacementOperandsRefused { reason: why },
      )
    ConvergenceOperandsAdmitted { operands: operands } =>
      placement_converge_with_operands(receipt_path: receipt_path, operands: operands)
  }
}

func placement_converge_for_spec(
  spec: DeploymentSpec,
  candidate: String,
  object_source: String,
  receipt_path: String,
) -> ProcessExit {
  match convergence_principal_admission(
    expected: spec.service.service_user as String,
    result: effective_posix_principal_observation(
      outcome: effective_posix_principal_read(read: EffectivePosixPrincipalRead {}),
    ),
  ) {
    ConvergencePrincipalMismatch { expected: e, observed: o } =>
      placement_finish(
        receipt_path: receipt_path,
        receipt: PlacementWrongPrincipal { expected: e, observed: o },
      )
    ConvergencePrincipalUnobservable { reason: why } =>
      placement_finish(
        receipt_path: receipt_path,
        receipt: PlacementPrincipalUnobservable { reason: why },
      )
    ConvergencePrincipalAdmitted { name: _ } =>
      placement_converge_admitted(
        spec: spec,
        candidate: candidate,
        object_source: object_source,
        receipt_path: receipt_path,
      )
  }
}

// THE RECEIPT PATH IS AN ARGUMENT AND IS ALSO DERIVED, WHICH IS NOT A CONTRADICTION. The unit
// renders it from the spec through convergence_receipt_path, so production never spells it; it
// remains a parameter so the acceptance matrix can point one execution at a scratch location
// without a second entry point that differs from the production one in any other way.
func repository_converge_srv1_owner_wet(
  candidate: String,
  object_source: String,
  receipt: String,
) -> ProcessExit {
  let spec = deployment_spec_srv1()
  if trim(s: receipt) == "" {
    exit_failure(reason: "--arg receipt=<path> is required -- the transition's outcome is written, not returned, because the deploy that ordered it runs in a different process")
  } else {
    placement_converge_for_spec(
      spec: spec,
      candidate: candidate,
      object_source: object_source,
      receipt_path: receipt,
    )
  }
}

// THE OTHER HALF OF THE READBACK: what the deploy runs after `systemctl start` returns. It is a
// separate entry point rather than a step inside the apply pipeline because binding it into that
// pipeline is the integration change this lane does not own.
type ConvergenceReadback
  = ConvergenceReadbackDecoded { receipt: ConvergencePlacementReceipt }
  | ConvergenceReadbackRefused { cause: String }

fn convergence_readback_holds(readback: ConvergenceReadback) -> Bool {
  match readback {
    ConvergenceReadbackDecoded { receipt: r } => placement_converged(receipt: r)
    ConvergenceReadbackRefused { cause: _ } => false
  }
}

fn convergence_readback_line(readback: ConvergenceReadback) -> String {
  match readback {
    ConvergenceReadbackDecoded { receipt: r } => placement_receipt_line(receipt: r)
    ConvergenceReadbackRefused { cause: c } =>
      join(["repository-convergence UNREPORTED -- ", c], "")
  }
}

fn convergence_readback_of_text(text: String) -> ConvergenceReadback {
  match decode_placement_receipt(text: text) {
    PlacementReceiptDecoded { receipt: r } => ConvergenceReadbackDecoded { receipt: r }
    PlacementReceiptUnreadable { cause: c } => ConvergenceReadbackRefused { cause: c }
  }
}

func read_convergence_receipt(receipt_path: String) -> ConvergenceReadback {
  let read = Filesystem.Read(path: receipt_path)
  if !read.success {
    ConvergenceReadbackRefused {
      cause: placement_receipt_read_refusal(path: receipt_path, error: read.error),
    }
  } else {
    convergence_readback_of_text(text: read.content)
  }
}

// THE READBACK'S ENTRY POINT, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE RESULT REACH THE OUTER TRANSACTION. The deploy
// runs this after `systemctl start` returns, and its ProcessExit is the transaction's answer about
// the repository -- not systemd's answer about the unit's control process. The two differ in exactly
// the cases that matter: a unit that started and then refused for a typed reason exits nonzero here
// with that reason NAMED, and a unit that appeared to succeed while writing no receipt is refused
// rather than assumed.
func repository_convergence_readback_wet(receipt: String) -> ProcessExit {
  if trim(s: receipt) == "" {
    exit_failure(reason: "--arg receipt=<path> is required -- the readback has no subject without it")
  } else {
    let readback = read_convergence_receipt(receipt_path: receipt)
    if convergence_readback_holds(readback: readback) {
      ExitSuccess
    } else {
      exit_failure(reason: convergence_readback_line(readback: readback))
    }
  }
}
